{"id": "q0284", "pool": "hard-knowledge", "n_q_chunks": 96, "onset_chunk": 92, "no_speak": false, "answer_text": "Okay, let's go through this step by step. So we have a contractor and homeowner signing a one- page contract for $ 5,0 00 to remodel the garage. Just before they sign, the homeowner points out that the written contract doesn't say when it will be done. They then have an oral agreement that it should be finished in 60 days, but that is not written into the contract. There' s no merger clause either. The contractor didn't finish on time, and now the homeowner sues for breach of contract. The question is whether the court can admit evidence about that oral agreement to finish in 6 0 days. Let' s look at what each option says.First, A says no because the oral agreement contradict s the written contract. But remember, there was never a time limit written down, so technically it doesn' t contradict anything yet; it just adds a new term. B says yes because the homeowner brought up the time limit before signing. That might matter, but it doesn't automatically mean the court has to allow the oral agreement. C says yes because the homeowner can provide evidence of the oral agreement. While true that they may have evidence, the key issue is whether the court is allowed to consider it under the law. D says yes because the time limit is an additional", "eot_seen": false, "n_ans_chunks": 60, "carrier_ended": null}
